  "notes": "The showroom is an interior fit-out on an upper floor of a red sandstone and brick commercial building on Aspen's pedestrian mall; the only exterior photograph is the street view, so the model is the host building: rusticated red stone ground storey under a stone band, red brick above, a corner block standing forward, a shaded recess with a balcony to its west, the tall open stair passage beside it, and the neighbouring shop with its wide multipane upper window. The interior views (tall many-paned windows looking across at old brick buildings, side windows on the flank wall) suggest the showroom is the upper floor of the corner block, which is where the side windows are put; which windows are its own is a guess. Depth, rear and roof of the building were not seen and are guessed. Winter: bare trees, snow beds along the mall drawn as white. Pass of 24 Sep 2026: the rusticated red stone is now sandstone (was corten), including the tall pier beside the stair passage and the string course over the ground storey; the pale stone bands are sandstone too, since the street view shows no pale trim; the lollipop trees are now tall cottonwoods \u2014 high trunks, two limbs, open mesh crowns \u2014 in the snow bed; the mall is dark pavers along the fronts, a snow bed with the trees, a path, and more snow beyond, as in the street view. Still guessed: everything the single street view doesn't show \u2014 depth, rear, roof, the east neighbour \u2014 and which windows are the showroom's. Refined 24 Sep 2026 against the street view: the punched windows now have their white frames (they were bare glass holes); the neighbour's wide upper windows carry the dark multipane grid of the photograph; a timber planter of evergreens and a patio heater at the foot of the stair passage, and the neighbour's blade sign. The stone and brick of the photograph are a darker maroon than the palette's sandstone and brick allow. Pass of 13 Oct 2026: the cottonwoods are now bare-limbed trees with grey bark (the photographs show tall pale trunks with open twig crowns, not mesh blobs); the neighbouring shop is a paler brown brick, as in the street view, not white render; the back-street windows have dark mullion and transom lines like the many-paned originals. Still too orange: the real brick and stone are maroon."
